From 4aec37a0155d45f2b927b568402d81bd8f771678 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arthur Sonzogni <arthursonzogni@chromium.org>
Date: Fri, 29 May 2026 12:37:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [Backport] Dependency for CVE-2026-121246

Cherry-pick done by Claude Code of patch originally reviewed on
https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/angle/angle/+/7885695:
GL: Extend RendererGL lifetime to fix SyncGL teardown UAF

When enabling PartitionAlloc locally or running under ASan, the test
MultithreadingTestES3.MultithreadFenceDraw/ES3_OpenGL (and other
multithreading tests) crash with a UAF during teardown.

The crash is caused by a lifecycle mismatch when cleaning up leaked
sync objects:
- The test loops exit early in the final iteration, leaking some
  GLsync objects (mSecondThreadSyncObj and mMainThreadSyncObj) in the
  shared SyncManager.
- During context teardown, Context::releaseSharedObjects() releases the
  ShaderProgramManager first, which destroys all programs. Since the
  programs hold the last std::shared_ptr<RendererGL>, RendererGL is
  deallocated.
- Subsequently, Context::releaseSharedObjects() releases the
  SyncManager, which performs a forced cleanup of the leaked sync
  objects.
- SyncGL::onDestroy() attempts to delete the syncs using a raw
  FunctionsGL* pointer, which points to the already-destroyed
  RendererGL, resulting in a UAF crash.

Why this happens despite DisplayGLX owning the default renderer:
- The multithreading tests use EGL_CONTEXT_VIRTUALIZATION_GROUP_ANGLE.
- For virtualized contexts, DisplayGLX::createContext creates a new
  RendererGLX instance and stores only a std::weak_ptr in
  mVirtualizationGroups.
- Thus, the lifetime of this virtualized RendererGLX is entirely
  bound to the contexts and programs using it.
- When the context and programs are destroyed during teardown, the
  refcount of the virtualized RendererGLX drops to 0 and it is
  deallocated immediately, before SyncManager::release() runs.

This patch fixes the lifecycle issue by modifying SyncGL to hold a
std::shared_ptr<RendererGL> instead of a raw FunctionsGL* pointer.
This ensures that RendererGL (and its function tables) is kept alive
as long as any SyncGL objects (including leaked ones) exist, making
the teardown robust and order-independent.

Alternative considered:
https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/angle/angle/+/7882142

Bug: b/517327192
Test: angle_end2end_tests --gtest_filter="MultithreadingTestES3.MultithreadFenceDraw/ES3_OpenGL" under ASan
